A||||11||ezsurf||Collision Fuel Shutoff?
Z||000000||ezsurf||07-05-99||07:23 PM||||<br>I finally got my engine in the car and ran it yesterday for about a half hour to break in the cam.  At the end of the run, eased off the gas to see if it would idle.  It worked it's way down to about 500 rpm and then died.  This morning I tried to start it again, but no luck.  I had to jack the car up to replace a battery cable, but thats all that was done to it between yesterday and today.  I talked with a mechanic and he said that if the car has a collision fuel shutoff, it may have been activated by jacking the car up.  I did some further trouble shooting and determined that the ignition is working fine, but the engine is not getting fuel. The fuel pump fuse is fine and you can hear the pump run.  I even pulled the cold start injector and took a look at it to see if there was a problem there, it looked fine.  I don't know if it's possible to vapor lock a MPFI system.  Does anyone know if this thing (86 SE) has a collision fuel shutoff?
Z||000001||Cooter||07-05-99||08:11 PM||||I dont think the Fiero had the pump shut off like you are talking. Check the pump relays on the firewall. YOu should be able to hear the pump run. Since you just built the motor, I would try to check the timing- there is a specific process to doing that I dont know because I have not put a light on my car yet. If the pump is running and still doesnt crank, just twist the distributer around some and see if it will hit. THis happend to a car I was working on and that was the whole problem-timing too low.
Z||000002||ezsurf||07-05-99||09:36 PM||||I had to adjust the timing to get the engine to start the first time in the manner you are suggesting.  This is not the problem at the moment. I removed a spark plug and visualy checked it.  The plug was dry.  I then sprayed starter  fluid in the intake.  It tried to start immediately.  If fuel was getting to the engine, it would have kept running.  I think the problem is the injectors, or the ecm controling them.  Anyone have any ideas?
Z||000003||theogre||07-05-99||09:43 PM||||there are some known issues with Fiero Fuel pumps.... on of them is that you can screw up the connector at the top of the tank if you shift the tank durring jacking or a big bump.<br>
Z||000004||Racer 1||07-06-99||12:51 PM||||Heres a simple test.Unplug your fuel inlet to you fuel filter.Then stick the fuel line into a empty soda bottle then tape the top of the soda bottle to the fuel hose.Then turn on the ignition till you here the pump come on.But dont turn over the engine as the vapors could explode and you could be a human fireball!
Z||000005||ezsurf||07-06-99||07:55 PM||||What are the odds?<br>When I did the engine run the other day, I planned to run the engine for 30 min to break in the cam.  After running the engine for 30 min exactly, I let off the gas to see if the engine would idle.  It went down to about 500 rpm and then died.  I tried all day yesterday to get it started with no luck. A mechanic determined the fuel pump was bad.  Actually there wasn't any !#@$%& fuel left in the $@!%#$ tank.  What are the odds you would have 30 min and 5 seconds of fuel in the tank?  The guage still indicates 4/10 full. Now the engine runs but the car won't go in gear.  This is becoming unfun.
Z||000006||four50four||07-06-99||08:07 PM||||well i hate to bring a honda in this thread but here goes: i ran my little dual-carbed prelude out of gas. it never started again. since the car was a peice of junk i hammered a hole in the back seat floor and ran direct wires to the battery fuel pump works, guage says quarter tank, but she will only fire. i disconnected a gas hose and turned the motor over, it spits a little bit of gas but not enough to keep it running. engine runs fine though cuz i put gas in the carbs and it fires right up so i think its the fuel pump or i'm out of gas and the guage is wrong, who cares shes headed to the scrap yard tommorrow! hope it helps
Z||000007||four50four||07-06-99||08:09 PM||||oops i guess my post was unneeded. just ignore it.
Z||000008||PaUL||07-07-99||07:46 PM||||Hey ezsurf, the collision fuel shutoff you are talking about works with the oil pressure sensor. If there is no oil pressure, or your sensor is disconnected the fuel is shutoff. I dont think the ECM controls this function, its a simple switch in series with the fuel pump. Thats why the sensor has 3 terminals, one for the gauge and 2 for the fuel pump. One comes from the voltage source the other leads to the fuel pump.
Z||000009||theogre||07-08-99||10:08 AM||||The ECM controls the fuel pump relay durring starting.
Z||000010||PaUL||07-08-99||02:00 PM||||Yes Ogre it does but the current supplied to the fuel pump relay goes through the oil pressure sender first. I know Im right on this one Ogre.
Z||000011||JetroGT||07-14-99||01:37 AM||||If you ran your car compleatly out of gas, you might want to change your fuel filter. Some particles in the bottom of the tank may have been sucked up and clogged it.
